WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.180
Before choosing a smart pointer, choose the ownership model.

2
00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:10.200
Exclusive ownership means one object or component is responsible for an object's lifetime.

3
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:14.380
That model is simple to reason about because cleanup has a clear owner.

4
00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:19.760
Shared ownership means more than one owner participates in keeping the object alive.

5
00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:26.020
It is useful in some object graphs and asynchronous designs, but it also makes lifetime harder to see.

6
00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.620
Weak observation is different from ownership.

7
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.560
A weak observer can check whether a shared object still exists without keeping it alive.

8
00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:42.500
This is valuable when callbacks, caches, parent-child relationships, or registries need a reference that should not control destruction.

9
00:00:42.600 --> 00:00:54.120
Borrowed access is also non-owning, but it is usually shorter lived: a function temporarily uses an object owned somewhere else and must not store that access beyond the contract.

10
00:00:54.400 --> 00:00:58.240
Value ownership is often the safest and simplest option.

11
00:00:58.340 --> 00:01:06.420
If an object can be a direct member, local variable, or element in a standard container, no dynamic smart pointer may be needed at all.

12
00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:15.240
Value ownership gives construction, destruction, and copying behavior that is often easier to inspect than a heap allocation hidden behind a pointer.

13
00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:19.440
Security review should resist pointer selection by habit.

14
00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:24.420
Do not use std::shared_ptr because passing it around feels convenient.

15
00:01:24.520 --> 00:01:30.600
Do not use std::unique_ptr when the real design requires stable shared lifetime.

16
00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:34.060
Do not use raw pointers to hide ownership.

17
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:41.280
The type should follow the actual lifetime relationship, and ambiguous ownership should be treated as a maintainability and safety risk.
